# EVERY EDGE OF EVERY GRAPH THIS MODEL COMPOSES, ONE ROW PER (graph, filing, from, to).
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ IT EXISTS BECAUSE FOUR FILES WROTE IT OUT BY HAND. `rank/cycle_space.sqlc`,
#    `rank/decomposition.sqlc`, `algebra/decomposition.sqlc` and the emitter each carried their
#    own copy of the same union over `composition/parts.sqlc` and `units/conversions.sqlc`, with
#    the same concatenation retyped in each. Four spellings of one relation is four chances to
#    typo one, and no law here could ever have compared them: an expression written inline is
#    not a left operand, so nothing could count it.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE NODE IS AN OPAQUE ID AND THAT IS THE ONE COMPROMISE IN THIS FILE. A layer graph's node
#    is `(filing, layer)` and a unit graph's node is `(unit)`, two different arities, and one
#    relation carrying both must pick a common type. Everything downstream of THIS relation
#    counts -- nodes, edges, components, two dimensions -- and a count needs only that distinct
#    nodes have distinct ids. ⭐ Which is why the flattening is legitimate here and was a defect
#    in the emitter: drawing needs to JOIN a node back to the model, and a concatenation cannot
#    be joined to anything. `rank/graph_nodes.sqlc` attaches each graph's node facts at real
#    grain and flattens afterwards, which is the same idiom in the order that works.
#
# ⚠️ THE SEPARATOR IS `/` AND NOTHING FORBIDS ONE IN A LAYER NAME. `pm:Layer/name` is an
#    `xs:token`, so two distinct layers could flatten to one node, and both the drawing and the
#    measurement would collapse identically and agree. Measured: 51 distinct `(filing, layer)`
#    pairs and 51 distinct strings, so it is injective by luck rather than by construction, and
#    this is the one place that has to be true rather than four.
#
# ⭐ `filing` IS THE PER-FILING CUT AND IT IS NOT PART OF THE EDGE'S IDENTITY. `rank/decomposition.sqlc`
#   asks whether cutting each graph by filing loses an edge, so it needs the column; `cycle_space`
#   takes the graph whole and ignores it. A caller that wants the corpus-wide edge set takes
#   `DISTINCT graph, from_node, to_node` and drops it, which is what both did inline before.
-- composition/parts.sqlc and units/conversions.sqlc, each labelled with the graph it is an edge of.
SELECT 'layers' AS graph,
       p.composition                            AS filing,
       p.composition  || '/' || p.composed_layer AS from_node,
       p.part_filing  || '/' || p.part_layer     AS to_node
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
UNION ALL
SELECT 'units', c.filing, c.from_unit, c.to_unit
FROM (
    :compose(units/conversions.sqlc)
) c
